`seo/page.loader.ts` uses local TanStack server/client environment functions.
There are no Website `createServerFn` wrappers, including for settings, and no
Website API proxy. Server loaders await private HTTP reads before rendering complete
page content and metadata. Browser loaders obtain the generated client supplied
by native `AtomHttpApi.Service` from the shared registry and run their request Effect
through the sole browser Promise bridge. The router's AbortSignal cancels that
read, while another caller and the shared client remain available.

Browser application data calls go directly to the checked same-stage public API
page and course GET endpoints. The Website calls its private API binding for initial HTML and its
existing non-HTML representations. There is one API publishing owner.

TanStack owns route loading, preloading, one-minute freshness, successful data
and `notFoundComponent`/`errorComponent`. There are no per-route request atoms,
custom success/error envelopes or Atom/Router cache synchronisation. Native HTTP
checks its own wire schemas and declared errors. The Website keeps application
checks for requested format, matching course identity and lesson references.
Typed failures and defects are translated once at each page, HTTP or tool
boundary; cancellation remains interruption. Server response adaptation retains
400/404/503 and uncached failures, with diagnostics kept outside loader data.
`toPageView` turns the Page's reading Option into a plain optional field while
preserving checked identifiers. Native TanStack serialisation handles that
successful view. The Website has no authored page records or image renderer.

The browser router creates one registry before loaders run. React uses that same
registry through documented `RegistryContext.Provider` and disposes it at final
root unmount. Native Atom scheduling cancels pending disposal during development
mount replay. The installed `RegistryProvider` creates its own registry, so it
is used only for the separate server-rendering subtree. Registry context stays
local and is never serialised as loader data.

`browserRuntime` owns local appearance/progress/analytics. `ApiClient` uses the
same factory for its native service runtime; `apiClientRuntime` applies native
`Atom.keepAlive` to retain that client until the registry closes. Native Layer
memoisation shares it with query atoms as well as route/tool calls. Root settings
initialise checked retained configuration atoms. FetchHttpClient omits
credentials and rejects redirects. Browser-specific tool handlers use the same
native client and the shared toolkit definitions; API handlers keep the actual
Content and Training services. Registration closes its scope on unmount and
does not start analytics. Query atoms are available for component-owned reactive
reads, rather than being a second cache for route data.

Native `FetchHttpClient.layer` retains its construction context through native
`HttpClient.layerMergedContext`, so the configured cookie and redirect settings
reach each request. Tests replace the native Fetch reference and inspect the
actual request options for browser reads and both analytics paths. No additional
client wrapper is needed to carry those settings.

`browser-atom.ts` reaches the browser module only inside TanStack's client
environment function; server rendering mounts inert atoms. No browser global is
read during import. The [native client implementation plan](docs/exec-plans/browser-actions-research.md)
records the replacement and its separate local, browser and deployment proof.

Appearance's checked choices, branded options, cookie readers/writers, browser
service and native Atom model belong to `@common-practice/appearance`. That
source-only package depends on Effect only. Its manifest and TypeScript settings
can be copied without repository catalogs or app configuration. It imports no
app, other workspace package, React, TanStack, Alchemy, artwork or CSS. The
Website's `appearance/settings.ts` supplies its existing cookie/channel names
and `appearance` attribute, preserving previously saved choices. The Website
owns colours, footer layout, server adaptation and cache policy. See the
[package guide](packages/appearance/README.md) for its public API and copy setup.

Both the Website and API use native Workers Cache scoped to their own Worker
versions. The API enables it with native `Cloudflare.cache` during construction;
Website.Vite declares its native cache prop. Successful
public HTML, page/course JSON, public Markdown and images make browsers recheck (`max-age=0`), while
Cloudflare may reuse them for seven days. Discovery text and the MCP card have
a five-minute browser lifetime. `Vary: Accept` separates HTML and Markdown
negotiation. Native API CORS retains `Vary: Origin`. API-catalogue,
OpenAPI, MCP and failures remain `no-store`. The isolate image cache is separate
from native response caching. Publishing RPC and its owned package are removed.
Static assets use Cloudflare's asset cache.

TanStack HTML additionally varies on the complete request Cookie header, so an
anonymous cached response cannot match a saved-appearance request. Valid saved
Light/Dark/Device choices use separate public variants, keeping the same initial
appearance and favicon. Malformed choices use `private, no-store` and CDN
`no-store`. Other cookies and their ordering can reduce HTML cache reuse. The
independent machine representations and assets keep their existing rules.
Stricter response-owned cache headers, Set-Cookie, failed responses and writes
cannot be widened into public shared storage. Seven days is freshness, not
guaranteed retention; Cloudflare may evict entries sooner. `stale-if-error=0`
prevents stale reuse beyond that period during failures. Both Worker versions
must refresh when content changes; the existing native Website input hash
includes content and API sources. Preview/production isolation, cache hits and
content-only release freshness need their own hosted proof.

Shared responses still require identical public content for every visitor.
Change this policy before adding sign-in, other personal content or data that
changes without deployment.
Local checks, hosted cache hits, browser behaviour and provider state remain
separate evidence. Dated records in `docs/evidence` never override this design.
